      "title": "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e0e01e62-6530-5bde-9db1-40b592f9f018",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime}$ given that $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨t^{2},\\,\\ln{(t )},\\,t^{2}⟩$ and $v(t)=- 3 t.$ Assume $t>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime}=v(t)\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)+v^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})=2 t \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\ln{(t )})=\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})=2 t \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨2 t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,2 t⟩ \\\\ v^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(- 3 t)⟨2 t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,2 t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 6 t^{2},\\,-3,\\,- 6 t^{2}⟩ \\\\ P_2 & =(-3)⟨t^{2},\\,\\ln{(t )},\\,t^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 3 t^{2},\\,- 3 \\ln{(t )},\\,- 3 t^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime} & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =⟨- 9 t^{2},\\,- 3 \\ln{(t )} - 3,\\,- 9 t^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
